{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And they drew and brought up.</b> At first they sold him while he was in the pit for only twenty pieces of silver, because his face had turned pale from fear of the snakes. And immediately 'they drew him' up to halfway in the pit, and immediately his handsome appearance returned to him once he was saved from the snakes. And when his brothers saw that he was beautiful as before, they did not want to give him for twenty pieces of silver, and they said to return him to the pit. And the Ishmaelites gave them in compromise more shoes, and this is what the verse says about their sale: ״על מכרם בכסף צדיק ואביון בעבור נעלים״ etc. And immediately 'they brought up Joseph from the pit' entirely." "<b>And they sold Joseph to the Ishmaelites.</b> Rashi explained that his brothers sold him to the Ishmaelites, and the Ishmaelites to the Midianites, and the Midianites to Egypt. And according to this, one may explain the passage thus: 'And they lifted their eyes and saw, and behold, a caravan of Ishmaelites was coming' — and meanwhile 'anashim passed by' etc. And therefore Scripture mentions them here, so that you not wonder when you arrive at the verse 'and the Midianites sold him to Egypt' — where did he come into the hands of the Midianites, and who sold him to them? And it informs you here that this is how the matter happened: while his brothers were discussing among themselves to sell him to the Ishmaelites, Midianite men passed by, and his brothers drew Joseph from the pit and sold him to the Ishmaelites — and all this the Midianites saw. And afterwards the Midianites acquired him from them, for they claimed: 'For our sake they brought him up from the pit in order to sell him to us'; and therefore they sold him to them, and they sold him to Potiphar. And if you ask: why is it written here 'and the Midianites sold' — it should have written 'and the Midianites' [with a yod]? Rashbam explained in his commentary on the Torah that Midianites and Medanites and Ishmaelites are one nation, even though Midian and Medan and Ishmael were brothers. And there is a difficulty, for above Rashi cited: 'Midrash Aggadah — פסים, after the name of his troubles, that he was sold four times.' And according to this, we find that he was sold only three times. Therefore it seems to me thus: 'And they lifted their eyes and saw, and behold, a caravan of Ishmaelites' etc., and they were discussing among themselves: 'Come, let us sell him to the Ishmaelites coming to us.' And before the Ishmaelites reached them, 'Midianite men, traders, passed by,' and they sold him to them while he was still in the pit, בעבור נעלים, in doubt whether he was alive or dead. And while they were drawing him from the pit, the Ishmaelites arrived. 'And the Midianites drew Joseph from the pit' and found him alive, and sold him to the Ishmaelites; and the Ishmaelites to the Midianites; and the Midianites to Egypt — here are four sales. And as for what is written later, 'And Potiphar bought him from the hand of the Ishmaelites' — this is how the matter happened: for when Potiphar saw him, he was astonished and said: 'A handsome man sold by a Cushite? And there is no Cushite selling a handsome man!' — 'גרומני' means a handsome person. And therefore he said: 'Surely this is no slave. Give me a guarantee that you did not steal him.' And the Ishmaelites who had sold him to them came and served as his guarantors. And therefore it is written מיד הישמעאלים, 'from the hand of the Ishmaelites,' because they were the ones who guaranteed him." "<b>For twenty pieces of silver.</b> And if you ask: how could a man as beautiful as Joseph be sold for only so little? One answer: he was sold for twenty pieces of silver to each and every one of his brothers. And in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer it asks thus, and answers that his face had turned pale from fear of snakes and scorpions. And some explain: בעשרים כסף — twenty of refined silver (זקוקים כסף), for the numerical value of the letters כסף equals one hundred and sixty (ק\"ס) asterli'ns [esterlins] of refined silver that they call mark, meaning that they are thirteen dinars and a half asterlin."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {2 "The note on gematria (כסף = ק\"ס = 160) is preserved as written; the compiler appears to treat the standard numerical value (160) and the notation ק\"ס (160) as interchangeable. The coin denominations (אסתרלינ\"ש, מאר\"ק, דינר') are rendered as they appear: asterlins/esterlins, mark, dinars."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:48.269776906Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}